response:
Problems found

1. Information leakage  
   • You explicitly return the password field (`password: user.rows[0].password`).  
   • `SELECT *` already contains the password column, so you are actually returning it twice.  
   • Any client that can hit the route can fetch every user’s password hash/​plaintext.

2. Missing authentication / authorisation  
   • Anyone who can hit `/api/users/:id` can read any user record.  
   • At minimum the caller should be authenticated and either (a) the same user that is being requested or (b) an admin.

3. No error handling  
   • If the query throws or `rows[0]` is undefined, Node will crash or Express will send a 500 with a stack-trace.  
   • When no user exists you should return 404, not crash.

4. Potential runtime error  
   • If the user id does not exist, `user.rows[0]` is `undefined`, so spreading it (`{ ...user.rows[0] }`) throws `TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object`.

5. No input validation  
   • `req.params.id` is accepted as-is. Although a parameterised query prevents SQL-injection in this statement, validating that it is an integer (and, ideally, positive) is still good practice.

6. Inefficient / unclear SQL  
   • `SELECT *` fetches all columns (including ones you don’t need) and relies on the table definition never changing.  
   • `LIMIT 1` should be added when you only expect a single row.

7. Missing rate-limiting / enumeration protection  
   • Attackers can iterate through `/api/users/1 … /99999` to harvest data.

8. Miscellaneous  
   • Returning a string id that the database may coerce internally can hide subtle bugs; cast it to an integer first.  
   • No logging of unexpected situations (DB failure, unauthorised access, etc).

How to fix

```javascript
// Example using Express and pg
const { check, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.get(
  '/api/users/:id',
  [
    // 1. Parameter validation
    check('id')
      .isInt({ min: 1 })
      .toInt()
      .withMessage('User id must be a positive integer')
  ],
  async (req, res, next) => {
    // 2. Handle validation errors
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }

    // 3. Authorisation example (pseudo-code)
    // if (req.user.id !== req.params.id && !req.user.isAdmin) {
    //   return res.status(403).json({ message: 'Forbidden' });
    // }

    try {
      // 4. Fetch only the columns that may be returned
      const { rows } = await db.query(
        'SELECT id, username, email FROM users WHERE id = $1 LIMIT 1',
        [req.params.id]
      );

      if (rows.length === 0) {
        return res.status(404).json({ message: 'User not found' });
      }

      // 5. Never send the password (we purposely did not query it)
      return res.json(rows[0]);
    } catch (err) {
      // 6. Centralised error handler receives the error
      return next(err);
    }
  }
);
```

Other hardening ideas  

• Add rate limiting middleware (e.g. `express-rate-limit`).  
• Enable a global error handler in Express to avoid leaking stack traces in production.  
• Log access to the route for auditing.  
• Use HTTPS and set appropriate security headers (Helmet).  
• Consider returning consistent error messages to reduce information disclosure.

Summary

– Do **not** return sensitive fields (password).  
– Validate input and authorise the caller.  
– Handle “not found” and database errors gracefully.  
– Query only the columns you need.  
These changes eliminate information leakage, prevent crashes, and improve maintainability and security.
